feat(openflow): resolve port_mac: in flow actions; name the PTAP blocker

Depends on 0001-controller-fix.patch (which passes the new port_macs argument);
apply 0001 first, then this. Neither compiles alone.

port_mac: resolution (new, working):
- resolve_mac() accepts either a literal MAC or `port_mac:<port name>`, looked
  up in the hw_addr map that 0001 harvests from the switch's own PortDesc reply.
  This is what lets openflow-static-flows.json say
  "eth_dst": "port_mac:ovsbr0" instead of baking a host-specific MAC into
  config that goes stale the moment the bridge is recreated. Used by SetField
  eth_dst/eth_src (dl_dst/dl_src aliases included).
- json_flow_to_add / json_flow_to_delete take &HashMap<String, [u8; 6]>.
  The delete path ignores it (deletes match on fields, not actions) but keeps
  the argument so both call sites in controller.rs stay symmetric.

encap / packet_type (recognised, refused with a reason -- NOT working):
The prio-200 netmaker flow cannot be encoded by this crate. Verified against
rovs-openflow 0.2.0:
- `Match` (match_fields.rs) has 43 fields, none of them packet_type, and
  `Match::encode` only emits fields it knows. There is no raw-OXM escape hatch,
  so OXM_OF_PACKET_TYPE (class 0x8000, field 44) is unreachable.
- `Action` has 25 variants, none of them Encap, and `ActionList::push` takes
  `Action` -- no raw/experimenter variant -- so OFPAT_ENCAP (29) is unreachable.

Rather than leave the flow failing with a generic "unsupported match field", it
is now parsed, validated and rejected with the exact missing crate support and
the out-of-band workaround (`ovs-ofctl -O OpenFlow15 add-flow`), so the operator
sees the real blocker in the log line 0001 emits as `skipping static flow`.
`JsonFlowAction::Encap { header }` (alias `ethertype`) exists for the same
reason: without it serde fails with an opaque unknown-variant error.

Unblocking this properly needs rovs-openflow to gain either packet_type +
Encap support or a raw OXM/action escape hatch; that is a change to
github.com/delandtj/rovs, not to this crate.

Tests: port_mac resolves to the discovered hw_addr, unknown port_mac is an
error (never a looser flow), the prio-200 netmaker flow reports the blocker and
the workaround, and packet_type syntax is validated before it is refused.
